**Abstract:** This paper examines the liquidity of HSBC shares between 1866 and 1898, and what determined that liquidity. HSBC was headquartered in Hong Kong, but its shares were traded on the London Stock Exchange. Using HSBC’s Register of Shareholders and the Investor’s Monthly Manual, I construct monthly liquidity measures and compare them with those of domestic UK bank shares. HSBC shares were persistently less liquid than comparable domestic shares, though the gap narrowed after the 1870s as the bank expanded and the telegraph developed in Asia. Trading in London responded strongly to British banking crises but was largely insensitive to those in Hong Kong. The trading was also influenced by the percentage of insiders among the shareholders. These findings show that information frictions continued to constrain the integration of colonial securities into the London market throughout the late nineteenth century.
